            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA released images showing **Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos**, as seen from the Martian surface. NASA/JPL says the sequence was captured by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z camera on **July 2, 2026**, and released **Aug. 5, 2026**. It’s described as the **first time humanity has captured Earth being occulted by another object from the surface of another planet**【5:1†source】.\n\nA few other recent Perseverance updates:\n\n- **New weekly rover imagery:** NASA’s raw-image page is current through **Week 285, July 26–Aug. 1, 2026**, showing the rover is still actively returning images【5:0†source】.\n- **“Broom Point” science results:** In mid-July, NASA reported that Perseverance found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** on Jezero Crater’s rim was built by repeated asteroid impacts【5:2†source】.\n- **Driving milestone:** Perseverance recently completed the equivalent of a **full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km** — reaching that distance on sol 1,890 of its mission, much faster than Opportunity reached the same milestone【5:4†source】.\n\nSo, in short: the headline today is Perseverance’s rare “Earth behind Phobos” observation, while the broader mission news is that the rover remains active, imaging, driving, and investigating ancient rocks beyond Jezero Crater.",
